      "math_academy_id": "q-42263",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 10 & 11\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -10 & -13\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 10 & 11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -10 & -13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =-2{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-2B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} & -2 & -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2 & -2\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & -2 & -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
